sections.content:
The model and the trap, verified by arithmetic:

```css
/* content-box (default) — the width you say is NOT the width you see */
.box { width: 200px; padding: 20px; border: 5px; box-sizing: content-box; }
/* rendered width = 200 + 20×2 + 5×2 = 250px  (arithmetic, not opinion) */

/* border-box — what you say is what you see */
.box { width: 200px; padding: 20px; border: 5px; box-sizing: border-box; }
/* rendered width = 200px — padding and border subtract from content */

/* the universal fix — the first line of most modern stylesheets */
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
```

The four layers, inside-out (the interview structure):
- Content: the text/media area; its size = width/height (or the content's natural size)
- Padding: clears space inside the border; takes the element's background (padding shows the background color/image)
- Border: the drawn edge; width, style, color; padding ends where border begins
- Margin: the invisible force field OUTSIDE; transparent; pushes OTHER elements; collapses with adjacent margins

The two facts that show real understanding:
1. Margin collapse (the classic gotcha): adjacent vertical margins collapse to the LARGER of the two — two stacked divs with margin: 20px have 20px between them, not 40. Padding never collapses. Flex/grid containers disable collapse in their children.
2. background coverage: background paints content + padding, NOT margin — a margin shows whatever's behind the element.
